# llvmdev — lift-source

Upstream: https://github.com/conda-forge/llvmdev-feedstock
Fork:     https://github.com/NAGAGroup/llvmdev-feedstock
Pinned:   07cac2ee5d1a79074b478602b825d10f3bc28a04 (conda-forge main, 2026-08-26)

LIFT, NOT SUBMODULE: we do not build this feedstock. Its content is copied
into our own core recipe, because acpp forces LLVM+clang into ONE cmake
configure and conda-forge's per-component split cannot carry it.

LLVM itself. WHAT WE LIFT: recipe/patches/0001-pass-through-QEMU_LD_PREFIX-SDKROOT.patch;
the two `sed`s at the top of build.sh that make osx behave like linux for
AddLLVM.cmake SONAME handling; and the flag set from its cmake invocation
(RTTI ON, LIBXML2/ZLIB/ZSTD FORCE_ON, INSTALL_UTILS, LLVM_UTILS_INSTALL_DIR,
EXPERIMENTAL_TARGETS WebAssembly, INTEL_JITEVENTS on linux-64, the osx
VERSIONED_DYLIB_NAME/UNVERSIONED_LIBLTO pair).

MEASURED FACT worth keeping: llvmdev builds ONLY llvm — no
LLVM_ENABLE_PROJECTS and no LLVM_ENABLE_RUNTIMES anywhere in build.sh or
bld.bat. Every other component is its own feedstock built standalone against
the installed LLVM. That split is exactly what cannot carry acpp.

Also: `-DLLVM_BUILD_LLVM_DYLIB=yes -DLLVM_LINK_LLVM_DYLIB=yes` on unix, while
bld.bat sets only -DLLVM_BUILD_LLVM_C_DYLIB=ON (the C API). That asymmetry is
the evidence behind "acpp cannot be a plugin on Windows".

